A||N||12||Formula88||Merchant's Tire Aligment Fiasco||||8||
Z||000000||Formula88||11-20-2001||06:17 PM||||Ok, I just spent the last 3 days trying to get my 88 Formula aligned (not maligned   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) at Merchant's Tire & Auto in Raleigh, NC.  I made sure to tell them it was a Formula, and as such, should get the 88 specs for Formula/GT, not coupe.  They said no problem.  Well, when I picked the car up, that's not quite what I had.  They did have the correct specs listed on the sheet, but that's not what it was set at:<P>Left Front.................Right Front<BR>Actual....Spec.............Actual....Spec<BR> 0.2...-0.5 - 0.5..Camber...0.5...-0.5 - 0.5<BR><B><FONT COLOR="red"> 2.7... 4.5 - 5.5..Caster...3.2... 4.5 - 5.5</B></FONT><BR> .11...0.05 - .25...Toe.... .12...0.05 - .25<P>Cross Camber:  Actual -0.3    Spec -0.8 - 0.8<BR>Cross Caster:  Actual -0.5    Spec -0.8 - 0.8<BR>Total Toe:     Actual 0.23    Spec 0.10 - 0.50<P>Left Rear....................Right Rear<BR>Actual....Spec...............Actual....Spec<BR><B><FONT COLOR="red">-0.1 ...-1.5 - -.5...Camber..2.0 ...-1.5 - -.5</B></FONT><BR>0.18 ...0.05 - 0.25...Toe...0.20 ... .05 - .25<P>Rear Total Toe: Actual 0.38    Spec 0.10 - 0.50<P>Ok.  You can see the front Caster is set to about 3*, which is correct for a coupe, not a Formula.  Also, the rear Camber is out of spec, possibly because I'm using 87 KYB's.  They tell me this is ok because the Caster up front is even, no matter that it's out of spec.  They make no mention of the rear, and I didn't catch it until after I left.  When I call them back, they said, no problem, bring it back and will get it right for you.  I leave the car with them all day, and at 4:30 they call me and tell me I need a Camber Kit to set the rear camber, for an extra $100 (parts & labor).  When I ask them about the front, they tell me that nothing extra is needed, as it's factory adjustable.  So I ask them why is wasn't adjusted correctly the first time.  All he says is 'cuz they were even.  <P>Am I nitpicking?  I want my car set up right.  The front caster may be OK, but that's not what Pontiac set it for.  They must have had a reason to change the spec from coupe to Formula/GT.  The rear could be because I'm using 87 KYB struts - but they didn't mention it the first time around.  <P>What do you think?  Should I pop for the camber kit?  Are they snowing me?  I kept getting the feeling they just wanted to do it "good enough", but not necessarily "right."  Well, it seems close now, so it should be ok to drive for a while, but I don't want to chew up my brand new tires and I want all the handling the car is supposed to have.  Suggestions?<p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||Pheagey||11-20-2001||06:30 PM||||my advice after dealing with garages to many time: They'll screw you if they can."<BR>go somewhere you trust (with ppl you know personally preferd). and no I dont think you are nit picking. One can not just do things 'enof' all the time or the world would be still in the stone age (Oga oga, stone work enof, no need metal. oga oga...)<BR>.<BR>anyways, My $0.02.<P>Have you seen my website yet? <A HREF="http://www.LithiumEffects.com/pheageyfiero" TARGET=_blank>http://www.LithiumEffects.com/pheageyfiero</A> <P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed.<BR>"The car formaly know as, but has returbned to being known as, Pheagey's Fiero."||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark||11-20-2001||06:53 PM||||Wow. It sounds like they didn't try very hard to adjust the rear camber.  And $100 for the "camber kit"?  Wow again.  This really seems steep.  I seem to recall these cam bolts go for about $20 per side. Here's a link to carparts.com... <A HREF="http://www.carparts.com/parts/sample.asp?reflink=45423-0-0-0-0&SegmentId=1&parttypeid=27010&vehicleid=45423&catalogid=&partnum=NONE&dc=" TARGET=_blank>http://www.carparts.com/parts/sample.asp?reflink=45423-0-0-0-0&SegmentId=1&parttypeid=27010&vehicleid=45423&catalogid=&partnum=NONE&dc=</A> <P>....so that only leaves them $60 labor to install these bolts!  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||11-20-2001||07:01 PM||||Well, they quoted me about $35 for the kit, plus $65 labor.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroMGDriver||11-21-2001||01:32 AM||||Just let me know which location this is so that I can keep my Fieros away.<P>------------------<BR>www4.ncsu.edu/~mgdickey/fiero.html||||reg||1
Z||000005||LZeitgeist||11-21-2001||02:16 AM||||This is the Merchant's on Millbrook Rd., near the intersection of Millbrook and Atlantic, across the street from the Gemini shop...<P>BTW - nice to meetcha - good to hear from yet another Fiero enthusiast in the Raleigh area...<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||11-21-2001||05:40 AM||||they were partly correct. Caster is what keeps the car rolling straight if your on a level road and you let go of the wheel. Also helps to pull the car back to straight after making a turn. As long as its close to correct, it shouldnt make a lot of difference as long as both sides are set to 'pull the same. Caster wont affect tire wear. fyi camber is when the top of tire is tilted in or out in relation to the bottom, toe is distance between tires at front of tire in relation to distance at the rear of tire when there pointed straight ahead. Camber and toe will def affect wear.||||reg||1
Z||000007||LZeitgeist||11-21-2001||06:58 AM||||However, caster makes a difference in 'feel' when performing at higher speeds. Less caster definitely makes the steering feel 'squirrily' as opposed to more caster, which brings the wheels back to center more smoothly on their own and keeps them there.<P>Being a Formula, the car was (or should have been) set for 5* at the factory - that's what was told to them before the service, that's what specs were shown *by their own records* before the service, and that's what they failed to set it at during the service. The car was brought in and paid for to set them to those specs - not for them to check it and declare them 'off the same amount on both sides, so it's O.K.'.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||11-21-2001||09:02 AM||||Yeah, what LZ said.  I could understand needing the camber kit on the rear end, but why didn't they tell me this in the first place?  The camber is clearly out of spec.  They didn't even mention that during the first visit.  As for the front caster, the guy was just lazy.  Ok, so he figured it was "good enough" the first time, but when I brought it back the second time, he still didn't fix it.  At first he told me it wasn't adjustable, then he told me it needed shims to be adjusted, then he told me it didn't need it, because they were equal on both sides.  I can forgive some lazyness, or hurriedness, cuz on Saturday, they were busy and shorthanded.  That's why I brought the car back on Tues. and left it for them all day.  That didn't help.  Right now the car seems to drive ok - it doesn't pull from one side to the other, but at high speed it feels a bit vague and squirrley on center.  Also, I'm sure the rear camber being out will accelerate my tire wear.  So now I STILL have to get the rear end aligned.  <P>Anyone with a Fiero, I encourage you to take your Fiero by the Merchant's Tire on Millbrook Rd., near Atlantic Ave., and let them know you won't be doing business with them because they do half-a$$ed work.  <P>I'm going to Greensboro for my alignments from now on - even if I have to tow the damn car there!  <B>Piedmont Truck Tire</B> has been recommended to me by both Bob Englert and Bhooper.  That's good enough for me.  I was originally going to take my car there, but was antsy about driving it 85 miles with the alignment so far out (newly installed struts).  Heck, they'll even let you sit in the car while they align it, so it's set perfectly with you in the car!  They did Hoop's GT, and it's been lowered, so I'm sure they can handle my stock height Formula.||||reg||4
Z||000009||Formula88||11-21-2001||09:09 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroMGDriver:<BR><B>Just let me know which location this is so that I can keep my Fieros away.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>See above.  And nice to see another Raleigh Fiero enthusiast.  You'll have to join us for one of our Fiero meetings sometime.  We should be having our next one either the 1st or 2nd weekend in Dec.  <P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/</A>  for some info on our little club.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <BR>You can email Bob Englert at renglert@triad.rr.com for meeting time and location information.<p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 11-21-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000010||Bob Englert||11-21-2001||12:49 PM||||Hello FieroMGDriver!<P>I will add you to our club e-mail list in case you would like to join us for lunch sometime.  We alternate between Raleigh, Greensboro and occasionally meet out west.<P>Our next meeting will be at Concord Mills Mall off I-85 on Saturday, December 8th.  <P>Hope to meet you soon.  That was a tough loss to Maryland last weekend...<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 11-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfiero||11-21-2001||01:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>Yeah, what LZ said.  I could understand needing the camber kit on the rear end, but why didn't they tell me this in the first place?  The camber is clearly out of spec.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You can adjust the rear camber without any eccentric cam bolts or "kits".  This shop is just being too lazy or they are ignorant on Fiero alignments, which isn't too unusual.<P>The struts have a slotted hole and a fixed hole......these are where the two large bolts go.  The bottom one is the slotted hole and is how camber is adjusted.  All this shop needs to do is loosen these two bolts and push in or pull out on the strut.<P>The "kits" make this job 100 times easier, but they are not necessary for a proper alignment.<P>The caster issue isn't a big deal, per se, but if the camber is out of whack (and they can't/won't fix it) then get your money back and go somewhere else.  <P>Don't waste $100 on that camber kit.....they are using that as an excuse for not being capable of aligning your car!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula88||11-21-2001||02:24 PM||||Well, all things being equal, I would tend to agree with you.  The one thing I'm not sure about is, I'm using KYB rear struts, which weren't designed for the 88, but fit, albeit just barely.  Some people have mentioned needing to elongate a bolt hole or do some grinding for clearance.  This could be the problem<P>You are right about the front, though.  That's just laziness.  <P>Anyone with KYB struts on the rear of their 88 care to let me know about any alignment issues they've had?  Is a camber kit the answer, or do I need to do some grinding?  Thanks! ||||reg||5
